{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Encapsulation"}, {"type": "position", "content": 2}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear and use to talk about encapsulation. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand key information about the concepts, principles, and methodologies in simple spoken and written situations.  Talk about programming techniques, use cases, kinds, and sequences in simple spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures.  Identifies general aspects related to Software Programming in short written or spoken conversations about past experiences."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob02re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Present Tense in All Forms"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use the <strong>simple present tense</strong> when we want to express regular situations and/or general facts. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Affirmative statements:</strong><br/><br/>  <div class=\"center_text\">Subject + <strong>verb</strong> + complement</div></br><br/>  Example: <span class=\"italic\">I <strong>design</strong> high-quality classes.<br/> She <strong>designs</strong> high-quality classes.</span><br/><br/>  Remember that when we use <span class=\"italic\"> he, she </span>, or <span class=\"italic\">it </span> in an affirmative statement, it is necessary to add <span class=\"italic\"><strong>\u2013s,\u2013es, or \u2013ies</strong></span> to the verb depending on its ending.<br/><br/>   Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">He teach<strong>es</strong> a class of XML at City University.<br/> This Virtual object run<strong>s</strong> only if your PC <strong>has</strong> Java installed.</span><br/><span class=\"italic\">He always tr<strong>ies</strong> new methods to design programs. </span><br/><br/>   <strong>Negative statements:</strong><br/><br/>  <div class=\"center_text\">Subject + <strong>do not (don't) / does not (doesn't) + verb</strong> + complement</div><br/><br/>  Example: <span class=\"italic\">I <strong>don't design</strong> high-quality classes.<br/>  She <strong>doesn't design</strong> high-quality classes.</span><br/><br/>  Remember that when we use he, she, or it in negative statements, it not necessary to add \u2013<span class=\"italic\"><strong>s,\u2013es, or \u2013ies</strong></span> to the verb because we have the auxiliary verb <span class=\"italic\"><strong>doesn't.</strong></span><br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">He <strong>doesn't teach</strong> a class of XML at City University.<br/> This Virtual object <strong>doesn't run</strong> if your pc <strong>doesn't have</strong> Java installed.</span><br/><br/>   <strong>Questions:</strong><br/><br/>  <div class=\"center_text\"><strong>Do / Does</strong> + Subject + <strong>verb</strong> + complement + ?</div><br/><br/>  Example: <span class=\"italic\"><strong>Do</strong> you <strong>design</strong> high-quality classes?<br/> <strong>Does</strong> she <strong>design</strong> high-quality classes?</span><br/><br/>  Remember that when we use <span class=\"italic\">he, she, or it</span> in questions, it is <u>not</u> necessary to add <span class=\"italic\"><strong>\u2013s,\u2013es, or \u2013ies</strong></span> to the verb as we have the auxiliary verb <span class=\"italic\"><strong>does</strong>.</span><br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\"><strong>Does</strong> he <strong>teach</strong> a class of XML at City University?<br/> <strong>Does</strong> this virtual object <strong>run</strong> on your PC?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Encapsulation allows us to enclose all those boxes, each one with different functions or subroutines."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We don't care about those things."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Do you worry about the gas tank?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Infinitives vs. Gerunds"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<strong>Gerunds</strong> are verbs in their -ing form. <strong>Infinitives</strong> are verbs followed by the preposition <span class=\"italic\">to</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Gerunds</strong> can be nouns if they are in the beginning of a sentence. You can recognize a <strong>gerund</strong> because it is a verb that ends in \u2013<strong>ing</strong>.<br/><br/>  Example: <span class=\"italic\">I like studying new methods to develop programs.</span><br/><br/>  When we use <strong>infinitives</strong>, we separate two verbs with the preposition <span class=\"italic\">to</span>.<br/> Example: <span class=\"italic\">I like to study new methods to develop programs.</span><br/><br/>  Sometimes we use <strong>gerunds</strong> or <strong>infinitives</strong> depending on the previous verb in the sentence, look at the next chart:<br/><br/>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":6,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"VERB + INFINITIVE\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"VERB + GERUND\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Ask\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Admit\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Choose\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Dislike\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Decide\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Imagine\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Like\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Practice\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Propose\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Spend\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example: <span class=\"italic\">I <strong>admit using</strong> platforms created by experts.<br/> I <strong>choose to generate</strong> the codes from scratch.</span><br/><br/>   <strong>Gerunds</strong> and <strong>infinitives</strong> can change the meaning of a sentence after some verbs such as prefer, intend, start, continue, begin, and some others."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"When they don't change the meaning \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> They intend to develop a new program.  <br/> They intend developing a new program. </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"When they change the meaning\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> I stop to check the details. <br/> I stop checking the details. </span>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Some programmers admit working with object-oriented languages is sometimes confusing, but they don't avoid it."}, {"type": "example", "content": "It keeps working without any modification from programmers or users."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I want to start this lecture by explaining an important part of the Object-Oriented Programming called encapsulation."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "user\u2013friendly "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "easy to use"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Encapsulation helps us to organize our programming in a better way to customize an application and make it user-friendly. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob02aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "avoid "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to prevent something from happening"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Some programmers admit working with object-oriented languages is sometimes confusing but they don't avoid it."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob02aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "end user"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the person who is going to use a software"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We have to keep it simple and show to the end user only what is fundamental to operate an application."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob02aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "customize"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to personalize or set up in such a way that satisfies the users' needs"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Encapsulation helps us to organize our programming in a better way to customize an application and make it user-friendly. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob02aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "keep it simple "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to do something in the easiest way possible"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We have to keep it simple and show to the end user only what is fundamental to operate an application."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob02aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "jargon "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "language used in a specific context or area"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: . I know there is too much jargon we have to keep reviewing."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob02aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Search more information about encapsulation."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write a list of the benefits of encapsulation for program development."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share the list with your class."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I list benefits of encapsulation"}, {"type": "text", "content": "I interpret and explain encapsulation."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my work with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "id": 0, "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob02re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "I want to start this lecture by explaining an important part of the Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) called encapsulation. To do so, imagine when you take a taxi. Do you worry about the gas tank? Do you care about the status of the engine? Well, we don't care about those things, the only thing we worry about is our destination. That's what Encapsulation is about. The idea is to focus on the functionality instead of the details that make the program work; sometimes we have to keep it simple and show to the end user only what is fundamental to operate an application."}, {"time": "35500", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob02re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "Nowadays, object-oriented programming makes things easy by joining everything in small \u201cboxes\u201d or classes, different from the past programming languages. Encapsulation allows us to enclose all those boxes, each one with different functions or subroutines, in order to hide details for users who don't need to know about them. Also, encapsulation protects the global variables and the structure of the program, and it keeps working without any modification from programmers or users."}, {"time": "66000", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob02re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Now, let's go back to the boxes I mentioned. These boxes contain both data structure and methods for manipulating the data. So, programmers identify these boxes and call them Classes. Some programmers admit working with object-oriented languages is sometimes confusing but they don't avoid it; instead, they find methods to make their life easier. To conclude, it is important to say that encapsulation helps us to organize our programming in a better way to customize an application and make it user-friendly. I know there is too much jargon we have to keep reviewing, but once you familiarize with all those terms, you will succeed in OOP."}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n2le03ob02re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n2le03ob02re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "title": ""}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can describe what encapsulation is."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can explain different facts related to encapsulation."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can understand what encapsulation is in different contexts."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What do you prefer, to create a program from scratch or to use a platform already created? Why?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What are the benefits of focusing on the functionality of a program instead of the details that make it work?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text",